  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA low pressure system associated with a triple point is sitting\njust off to our south slowly traversing east. This feature has \na trough along the mountains which is promoting some instances \nof light rain across the higher elevations. Among the rain, low \nclouds are prevalent along the feature as well. As this \nmeandering frontal boundary associated with the parent low \npressure system lingers about, the rest of the weekend could \nlook at chances of rain shower activity across the southern \nportions of the CWA. This feature along with another wave of low\npressure forecast to ride along the boundary will maintain \nchances of rain showers confined to this sector of the CWA \nthrough Sunday afternoon. \n\nBy Sunday afternoon, a low pressure system traverses across the\nGreat Lakes and brings a reinforcing moisture starved cold \nfront through prompting the boundary to our south to push off \nand out of the region to where chances of rain diminish. This \nwill also prompt cooler and drier conditions across the entire \narea going through midweek. A chance for frost is possible in \nthe northeast mountains this Tuesday morning and even possibly \nWednesday morning. Enough surface flow will be suppressed along \nwith low cloud coverage to initiate some frost development where\nvalleys drop in the mid to upper 30s across the northeast \nmountains. \n\nHigh pressure will maintain settled weather for the rest of the\nweek and possibly beyond with below seasonable temperatures \nexpected. The next chance for precipitation will be next \nSaturday with only a slight chance of rain along the mountains.",
